{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-assumption-or-perception",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "discrimination by assumption (or perception)"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "Discrimination that occurs when an individual is treated unfavourably because they are perceived, presumed, or stereotyped as having a protected characteristic, even if that perception is incorrect."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "What matters is the discriminator’s belief or assumption, not the person’s actual status. This type of discrimination acknowledges that prejudice often arises from perception and stereotype, rather than factual attributes. Equality law therefore protects individuals against being targeted due to mistaken beliefs about their identity. Whether the attribute assumed is “real” or “misattributed” is irrelevant; the harm lies in being subjected to biased treatment because of what others assume about one’s identity. The CRPD, by extending protection to persons with impairments or perceived impairments, also implicitly embraces this approach (Article 1 and Article 5)."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "6.3.3"
  ],
  "broader": {
